    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p142-q48-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $f(g(\\pi))=1$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1$. Since $\\pi$ is irrational, $g(\\pi)=0$ and $f(0)=1$.",
          "worked": "Since $\\pi\\notin\\mathbb Q$, the second branch gives $g(\\pi)=0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(g(\\pi))&=f(0)\\\\f(g(\\pi))&=1\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p142-q48-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that $f\\!\\left(g\\!\\left(\\dfrac{22}{7}\\right)\\right)$ is undefined.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Undefined over $\\mathbb R$. Since $\\frac{22}{7}\\in\\mathbb Q$, $g\\!\\left(\\frac{22}{7}\\right)=\\frac{22}{7}>1$, outside the domain $[-1,1]$ of $f$.",
          "worked": "Since $\\frac{22}{7}\\in\\mathbb Q$, the first branch gives $g\\!\\left(\\frac{22}{7}\\right)=\\frac{22}{7}$. The radicand is\n\n$$\\begin{align*}1-\\left(\\frac{22}{7}\\right)^2&=1-\\frac{484}{49}\\\\1-\\left(\\frac{22}{7}\\right)^2&=-\\frac{435}{49}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe radicand is negative, so $f\\!\\left(g\\!\\left(\\frac{22}{7}\\right)\\right)$ is undefined over $\\mathbb R$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p142-q48-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Find the domain and range of $f(g(x))$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p142-q48-c-short-domain",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "D=[-1,1]\\cup(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\mathbb Q)",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p142-q48-c-short-range",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "R=\\left\\{\\sqrt{1-q^2}:q\\in\\mathbb Q\\cap[-1,1]\\right\\}",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p142-q48-c-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Separate rational and irrational inputs. Irrational inputs give ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "1",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", already included when ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "q=0",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "For irrational $x$, $g(x)=0$ and $f(g(x))=1$. Every irrational input is therefore permitted.\n\nFor rational $x$, $g(x)=x$. The outer square root requires $1-x^2\\ge0$, so $-1\\le x\\le1$.\n\nThus\n\n$$D=[-1,1]\\cup(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\mathbb Q).$$\n\nThe rational inputs produce exactly the values $\\sqrt{1-q^2}$ with $q\\in\\mathbb Q\\cap[-1,1]$. Irrational inputs contribute only $1$, which is already obtained from $q=0$. Hence\n\n$$R=\\left\\{\\sqrt{1-q^2}:q\\in\\mathbb Q\\cap[-1,1]\\right\\}.$$\n\nThis range is not the whole interval $[0,1]$. For example, an output of $\\frac12$ would require $q=\\pm\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$, both irrational, so neither is an admissible rational input.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      }
    ]
  },
